// t_3e342903, consumer half: a float4 literal used as the operand of a
// matrix multiply must keep all four of its lanes.
//
// The filed symptom had two parts.  The CONSTANT POOL half - a float4 literal
// keeping only its .x - is pinned by vp-literal-vector-test.sh, which decodes
// the VPO parameter table and requires two distinct CG_FLOAT4 slots with all
// four values present.  What that fixture cannot see is the CONSUMER: whether
// the instruction reading the literal reads it as a vector or as a broadcast
// of one lane.  This shader is that half, judged on pixels.
//
// THE RESULT IS WRITTEN TO TEXCOORD1 AS WELL AS COLOR, and that is the whole
// reason this fixture is shaped the way it is.  The rig's VP control
// (control-vp-mismatch) demonstrates a difference on tc1 - 4096 pixels - and
// on no other channel, so tc1 is the channel a boot PROVES the judge can see.
// A row whose only evidence sits on a channel no control exercises is a green
// that has not earned anything; writing to both means the verdict rests on the
// proven channel and col0 comes along as corroboration.
//
// THE FOUR LANES ARE DELIBERATELY DISTINCT AND UNORDERED.  With a broadcast of
// .x the result is M * (k, k, k, k), a different value in every lane for an
// auto-bound matrix.  Measured against the matrix the stager binds for this
// row, a broadcast moves GREEN by 11 levels, BLUE by 4 and ALPHA by 3.
//
// RED IS BLIND HERE, by arithmetic rather than by accident: red's delta is
// row 0's OFF-DIAGONAL terms times the lane differences, and the auto matrix
// is diagonal-dominant, so red reads the same either way whatever order the
// literal's lanes are written in.  This is a three-lane witness - green, blue
// and alpha - and must not be read as a four-lane one.
//
// The scale keeps every value inside 0..1: four accumulated products of
// auto-bound values reach ~1.6 unscaled, and a clamped lane is a lane where
// correct and broadcast can agree.
void main(float4 p : POSITION, uniform float4x4 M,
          out float4 position : POSITION,
          out float4 color : COLOR,
          out float4 tc1 : TEXCOORD1)
{
    position = p;
    color = mul(M, float4(0.404f, 0.186f, 0.277f, 0.349f)) * 0.25f;
    tc1 = color;
}
